</pdd-interface>

<pdd-dependency>prompts/shared/config_python.prompt</pdd-dependency>

Implement `orchestrator/publisher.py`. It runs after a successful compile: it pushes the target app,
waits for Render to serve that specific build, and then posts this system's own
`POST /webhooks/render`.

<responsibility>
Get the compiled app in front of people, and report the moment it is genuinely serving — the build we caused, not merely a healthy site.
</responsibility>

<non_responsibilities>
This module DOES NOT compile. `compiler.py` produced the source; this publishes it.
This module DOES NOT select features or read `feature_requests`. It is handed the ids the build carried.
This module DOES NOT write `deployments` or move a feature to `COMPILED`. The webhook endpoint owns both, so a manual Render deploy and an automated one converge on exactly one code path.
This module DOES NOT block the sprint. The caller starts it and moves on.
This module DOES NOT trigger a Render deploy through the API — the git push does that, which is what Render is configured to watch.
</non_responsibilities>

<vocabulary>
Successful: of a publish, that our deploy reported `live`. Of a compile, something that already happened before this module was called.
Safe: of a repeated webhook post, that the endpoint is idempotent on `render_deploy_id` so no duplicate row is written. Safe is not a reason to retry.
Our deploy: the one deploy this push caused, identified by an id absent from the pre-push snapshot whose `commit.id` equals the sha we pushed. Neither half is sufficient alone.
Terminal status: `live`, `build_failed`, `update_failed`, `canceled`, `pre_deploy_failed`, `deactivated`. Everything else (`created`, `queued`, `build_in_progress`, `update_in_progress`, `pre_deploy_in_progress`) means keep waiting.
Live: our deploy reporting `live`. Not "the site returns 200" — the previous build answers 200 the entire time a new one is building.
</vocabulary>

<capabilities>
- MAY run git through the injected runner, call the Render API through the injected `get`, and post the webhook through the injected `post`.
- MUST NOT read or write Postgres, publish to Redis, or call a model.
- MUST NOT import `sprint_service` or `compiler`; the caller sequences them.
</capabilities>

<contract_rules>
R1 (MUST): Identify our deploy as one whose id is **absent from a snapshot taken before the push** *and* whose `commit.id` matches the sha we pushed. Neither test alone works: the site is `live` on the old build throughout a new build, and the same commit is legitimately deployed many times — a real service history shows five deploys sharing one sha, two of them `build_failed`. Matching on sha alone would report a week-old failure as this build's outcome.
R2 (MUST): Take the snapshot **before** pushing. Afterwards it is impossible to tell which deploys predate us.
R3 (MUST NOT): Treat "the preview URL answers 200" as evidence of anything. The previous build serves the whole time; this is precisely the confusion R1 exists to remove.
R4 (MUST): Read the deploy list from `GET https://api.render.com/v1/services/{RENDER_SERVICE_ID}/deploys`, authenticated with `Bearer {RENDER_API_KEY}`. Each list item is wrapped as `{"deploy": {...}}`, and the fields used are `deploy.id`, `deploy.status`, `deploy.commit.id`, `deploy.createdAt` — verified against the live API, not inferred.
R5 (MUST): Poll on a bounded interval until our deploy reaches a terminal status or the timeout expires, and treat the timeout as a failure rather than assuming success. A build that never finishes has not shipped.
R6 (MUST): Treat only `live` as success. `deactivated` means a later deploy superseded ours, which is not this build going live.
R7 (MUST): Tolerate our deploy not appearing immediately — Render takes a moment to register a push. Keep polling until the timeout rather than concluding on the first empty result.
R8 (MUST): Post to `settings.SELF_API_BASE` + `/webhooks/render` — **not** `FORUM_ORIGIN`, which is the frontend's browser origin and answers `404` for this path, and not `SANDBOX_URL`, which is the deployed target app. This has already cost one live run. Post to this system's own `POST /webhooks/render` on success with a body whose keys are exactly `event`, `render_deploy_id`, `version`, `preview_url`, `feature_ids` — the discriminator field is **`event`**, not `type`; the endpoint and the frozen `openapi.yaml` both read `event`, and any other name means our own webhook rejects our own post. Send `event: "deploy_live"` on success and `event: "deploy_failed"` with the same ids on failure so the features return to `VOTING` instead of stalling in `IN_SPRINT`.
R9 (MUST): Send the secret in the header the endpoint actually reads — `X-Webhook-Secret`, which is what FastAPI derives from its `x_webhook_secret` parameter. Inventing a name such as `X-Render-Secret` is a silent `401` from our own endpoint; this has already cost one live run. Never log it, and MUST NOT log it, the API key, or any other credential.
R10 (MUST): Use `settings.SANDBOX_URL` as the `preview_url`. The webhook validates it against the allowlist, so a value from anywhere else would be rejected by our own endpoint.
R11 (MUST): Derive `version` from the deploy — its short commit sha is the honest answer, because that is what Render served.
R12 (MUST NOT): Retry the webhook post more than once. The endpoint is idempotent on `render_deploy_id`, so a retry is safe but pointless; report failure and let the operator see it rather than looping.
R13 (MUST): Push by invoking the target repo's own `deploy.sh` through the injected runner with `SKIP_COMPILE=1` set and `SKIP_PUSH` **explicitly removed from the child environment** rather than merely unset in this process — a daemon started from a shell that had it exported would otherwise never push, and would report success having shipped nothing. Do not reimplement the script's logic: and MUST NOT reimplement its commit-and-push logic. That script is what the target project maintains; duplicating it here means two things to keep in step.
R13a (MUST): Set `SKIP_COMPILE=1` when invoking `deploy.sh`. `compiler.py` has already produced the source from the blueprint, and the script recompiles by default — leaving it unset pays for a second pdd call per cycle, adds minutes to every publish, and hands the compiler model a second opportunity to re-roll the app's whole appearance behind the blueprint's Design Lock. The publisher publishes what was compiled; it does not compile.
R14 (MUST): Return the pushed commit sha from the push step, since R1 depends on it. Fail loudly if it cannot be determined — polling for an unknown sha would match nothing and time out looking successful in the logs.
R14a (MUST): Use the same `Runner` shape as `compiler.py` — `(command: str, cwd: Path, env: Mapping[str, str]) -> (returncode, stdout, stderr)`, positional. Two runner signatures in one codebase means every future module picks one at random and every test has to know which.
R15 (MUST): Accept every outbound call as an injected seam (`runner`, `get`, `post`), so the whole sequence is testable without a network, a git remote, or a Render account. The **defaults must use `httpx`** — it is the only async HTTP client installed; `aiohttp` is not a dependency, and reaching for it means the module imports fine and then fails the first time it is actually used.
R16 (MUST NOT): Raise out of `publish`. Return a `PublishOutcome` describing what happened. The caller is a pipeline step that must keep going.
R17 (MUST): Log each transition — pushed, deploy identified, status changes, terminal outcome — at info, without credentials. This is the step most likely to be watched live during a demo.
</contract_rules>

<pdd>
**Why this is not part of `sprint_service`.** The sprint selects one winner and must stay responsive:
its own contract says it neither compiles nor deploys, and it holds a Redis lock while it runs.
Waiting minutes for a Render build inside that lock would block every later sprint and make a slow
build look like a wedged system. Keeping the publisher separate lets the caller fire it and move on,
which is the asynchrony that matters — the sprint returns as soon as the push is away.

**Why the webhook rather than writing `deployments` directly.** A manual `./deploy.sh` and this
automated path then converge on one code path with one set of validations, and the webhook already
owns idempotency, the allowlist check, the `COMPILED` transition and the ticker announcement. A
second writer would have to keep all of that in step.

**`deactivated` is deliberately a failure.** It means a newer deploy replaced ours before it went
live, so reporting it as success would mark features shipped on the strength of a build nobody ever
served.
</pdd>

